my name is Devon Brewer and I'm going to  talk about strategies for successful  grant proposals  because I will give advice about how to  write grant proposals let me tell you a  little about a little bit about my  background I have over 25 years of  experience in writing successful grant  proposals in the Health Sciences  I have been a principal investigator on  several grants and a key contributor to  many others I have written funded  proposals or reviewed proposals for the  National Institutes of Health the  National Science Foundation the Centers  for Disease Control and Prevention and  the health resources and Services  Administration the Medical Research  Council private foundations and other  agencies  as a scientific adviser and writer with  science stocks I help researchers  develop and compose effective proposals  and manuscripts  in this tour of writing grant proposals  I'll start with what I think is an  essential mindset for the whole  enterprise this mindset leads to  specific actions to ensure the content  and presentation of a proposal matches a  grant opportunity  then I'll highlight some logistic and  bureaucratic matters to address for an  effective submission I'll close with  some suggestions about how to build  skill and developing and writing grant  proposals and where you can get feedback  on in help in preparing proposals  although most of the examples I give  relate to funding funding agencies in  the United States the strategies I  discussed are you likely to be useful in  other countries as well  to have an effective mindset for writing  a successful grant proposal it helps to  understand two basic facts of the  research grant game  the first is that thunders give grants  to advance their missions which usually  are of a practical political economic or  ideological nature  a project that is not aligned with the  funders agenda will not get funded no  matter how excellent important or  creative the science is  the second basic fact is that scientists  who apply for research grants and  contracts therefore our vendors offering  research projects for sale  so a research grant is not a handout  just to advance your career or satisfy  your curiosity although you might be  able to achieve these while benefiting  your funder  you are selling your research it pays to  follow the first rule of sales no your  customer but who are your customers  they are the review panel and funding  agency officials  the review panel is an intermediary and  you have to make your offer appeal to  their agendas - they will try to a  certain extent to adhere to agency  priorities and views but they will also  have their own that they seek to apply  with reviewing proposals  and reviewers may vary in their  individual agendas as well  you  there are several strategies you can use  to know your customers with respect to  the content of your proposed research  usually you have an idea for a research  project and want to seek funding for it  in this case the first step is to search  for potential funders consider all types  of possible sponsors including  government agencies private foundations  industrial corporations patient advocacy  groups and other nonprofit organizations  grants.gov has a directory to all  federal funding agencies I've given the  link here  search the web intensively for non  government funders  identified a potential thunder pick the  most relevant grant opportunities or  programs  I've given the links to listings for the  National Institutes of Health and the  National Science Foundation examine the  alternatives thoroughly look for good  fits in terms of topic and size of  project  if you don't have an idea to begin with  reviewing funding opportunity  announcements or fos may spark an idea  if you already had an idea you may  decide to modify it in light of these  announcements  next identify the specific target for  your proposal within the funding agency  study previous grants made by the  program or programs in which you're  interested  I've given links to the relevant  databases for the National Institutes of  Health in the National Science  Foundation  if you plan to submit the National  Institutes of Health also examine the  emphases and rosters of the review  panels called study sections  search for publications by the reviewers  and visit their web pages to understand  their specialties and inclinations  these sources of information will help  you decide precisely which program fo a  and review panel is best for your  project  try to find the program and review panel  that is most similar to your idea in  substitute methodological and  theoretical terms avoid programs and  review panels that are likely to be  hostile to or unfamiliar with your  approach and perspective  once you have identified your target  program in fo a or are still uncertain  email and then call the program officer  to discuss your idea the program officer  may give you a sense of the program's  current priorities and the tendencies of  the review panel this is as close as you  can get to communicating with your  ultimate customer take seriously what  the program officer tells you  all this you may want to revise your  idea further based on what you have  learned  in the proposal itself make sure you  highlight how your project will address  one or more topics listed in the FO a if  they were specified as you write take  the agency's perspective show how your  work connects to the funders priorities  and goals don't propose research to a  government agency that is difficult or  impossible to fund for political reasons  don't propose research to other funders  that is it odds with their ideologies  try to make your proposal seem like it  is within the same stream of research as  that of the reviewers and recent  grantees  reviewers can be very tribal and they  want to feel like they are supporting  one of their own  integrate your proposed project with  their work and sighted  early revolutionary ideas are not  fundable most scientists will not break  entirely with convention or current  understanding without evidence and a  proposal is merely a plan to gather  evidence  individual scientists can take such  risks on their own but funders generally  do not you still can come you still can  propose something new or different as  long as it doesn't challenge  foundational beliefs  reviewers and funders want to support  successful projects so include any  preliminary data and results you have  related to your proposed research to  demonstrate the viability and potential  outcomes for your project  proposal is a sales document maintain  scientific integrity throughout describe  prior work fairly give others credit  where it is due ensure all claims have a  factual basis and avoid hyperbole  include best practices such as  pre-registration of study protocols and  public archiving of data in your plans  acknowledged limitations and state how  you will address them or why the project  is valuable despite them  the content of other sections beyond the  main text are important too unless the  grant is small or for new investigators  at least one of the main investigators  on the project should have considerable  research experience in the area of the  proposal a track record  typically demonstrated by relevant  publications is crucial for all key  researchers on the project  show that you are ready to begin the  project identify by name if possible who  will fill each major role rather than  indicating the person is to beat to be  determined exclude individuals with  nominal involvement for tiny percentages  of time unless they feel very specific  and limited technical roles  budgets are often scrutinized by  reviewers and funders alike regardless  of the detail required in the proposal  over estimation of a budget can be a  sign of inexperience or attempted abuse  of grant funds  underestimation of a budget can also be  a sign of inexperience and ignorance  the Thunder requires a description of  specific costs justify exactly how they  are necessary for the project and the  project alone  many funders cut grantees budgets after  awarding their grants for reasons  unrelated to those specific projects so  be ready with a back-up plan of how you  would achieve your project goals with  for example a 10 percent or 25 percent  cut tier budget  knowing your customer also applies to  how you present your proposed project  if you do not prepare your proposal well  it has no chance at funding no matter  how good your content is  how well you prepare the proposal  reflects your attention to detail  ability to adhere to procedures and  communication skills which are all  essential for carrying out a successful  research project therefore follow all  application instructions exactly  consider your reviewers constantly as  you write and design your proposal few  reviewers will be expert on your  specific topic and many might even be  from entirely different fields sometimes  only one or two reviewers give comments  on a proposal so chance may figure  promise prominently and who will review  your proposal  remember that reviewers are tired and  overworked volunteers in a rush  these circumstances have several  implications for how you will compose  your proposal  because you cannot know the extent of  the reviewers knowledge explain your  assumptions and all specialized concepts  techniques theories and terms  the project summary or abstract in the  specific game section in National  Institutes of Health proposals are the  most important parts of the proposal  text these are the only parts some  reviewers will read and full as they  skim the rest for all reviewers these  parts set the tone  good first impressions can be lost that  bad first impressions are very difficult  to overcome  all scientific writing brevity is  critical reviewers have other proposals  to read and unnecessary words and  material hurt your case  organize and format your proposal in  simple clear and consistent ways the  reviewers can navigate and find  information easily avoid using text  effects such as bolding and underlining  within paragraphs  these effects imply some words and  sentences are more important than others  which readers can interpret his meaning  the others can be skipped  considerable planning and coordination  with administrators are required to  produce a successful proposal expect to  spend at least several months developing  and preparing all aspects of your  proposal writing a grant proposal is not  like writing a research article it is  more like writing two or three long  research articles in terms of time and  effort involved  we humans especially scientists writing  grant proposals are chronic under  estimators of the time needed to  accomplish tasks remember Hofstadter's  law it always takes longer than you  expect even when you take into account  Hofstadter's law  schedule for when you and others will do  different tasks for the proposal  building extra cushions of time and hard  deadlines stick to the schedule  if you fall too far behind be prepared  to postpone submission to a later grant  cycle or another opportunity  once you have identified the funder and  grant opportunity you will seek make  sure you are eligible to serve as  principal investigator and that your  organization is eligible to receive the  grant  check that the grant duration and  overall cost allowed fits with your  project ideas  if your organization is new to seeking  research grants especially from  government funders there may be various  registrations and accounts that your  administrators will need to establish  also your proposal schedule should  include all relevant deadlines set by  the funder  the administrative aspect of a proposal  is just as important as a scientific  aspect once you decide to develop a  proposal meet with administrators at  your organization  figure out who will be responsible for  each component of the proposal and every  task related to it identify internal  deadlines  incorporate the division of labor and  these internal deadlines into your  proposal development schedule  good relationships with administrative  staff are crucial for preparing a  successful proposal if possible be  concise and guidance from experienced  grant administrators they can help you  avoid bureaucratic problems both with  the Thunder and your own organization  prioritize getting the necessary  approvals from your organization's  biosafety Animal Care or Human Subjects  committees you may need to revise your  planned project and go through multiple  committee meeting cycles before your  proposal is approved  the best way to learn how to write  successful grant proposals is to  apprentice with experienced writers of  successful grant proposals  if this is not possible for you then  volunteer to proofread a review or  proposal by a colleague who has received  grants before  another option is to ask colleagues  whether you can study successful grant  proposals that they've written  in a grant proposal you are not only  selling a research project you are also  selling yourself you stand a better  chance at funding if you are a known  contributor to the field  therefore presented conferences publish  your work and agree to review  manuscripts in your field before you  develop a proposal if possible  reviewers and funders are putting their  trust in you just as much as they are  your idea and plan  getting feedback on  proposal maybe even more important than  getting feedback on a manuscript because  a proposal is a plan that still can  change while a manuscript is a report of  something that already happened  at a minimum have all key scientific  personnel proofread and review the whole  proposal ask other colleagues to review  the proposal as well and seek their  feedback on your ideas even before you  begin writing  there are also other resources you can  draw on at science stocks we help  researchers and all phases of developing  and writing grant proposals and  manuscripts we have experts in every  specialty ready to assist you can learn  more at science.com  thank you for your attention
 

